tree-sitter 0.27.1: changed ranges after the iterators are re-aligned by depth

ts_subtree_get_changed_ranges walks the old and new trees side by side. When its two
iterators end up at different depths at the same position, the deeper one is moved up to an
ancestor, and the next round compares that ancestor with the other tree's node from the
middle of the ancestor. iterator_compare can then declare the two identical (same symbol,
start, size and error cost, no edit recorded in the old one, equal scanner state on the last
external tokens passed), and the walk jumps to the ancestor's end: the part under the
current position, which differs in depth, is never reported.

Seen with tree-sitter-haskell and this grammar: an error at the top of the file leaves the
layout scanner in a state that puts a final #endif inside the instance body. Undoing the
error moves #endif back out, both trees are right for their text, and the changed ranges
between them are empty.

A match is never trusted right after a re-alignment; the walk descends instead. The common
edit path is untouched: an ancestor that contains the edit has changes recorded and is
descended into already. Drop this patch when tree-sitter-runtime.txt moves to a release
that includes the fix.

diff --git a/lib/src/get_changed_ranges.c b/lib/src/get_changed_ranges.c
--- a/lib/src/get_changed_ranges.c
+++ b/lib/src/get_changed_ranges.c
@@ -423,6 +423,7 @@ unsigned ts_subtree_get_changed_ranges(
   Iterator new_iter = iterator_new(cursor2, new_tree, language);
 
   unsigned included_range_difference_index = 0;
+  bool realigned = false;
 
   Length position = iterator_start_position(&old_iter);
   Length next_position = iterator_start_position(&new_iter);
@@ -445,6 +446,8 @@ unsigned ts_subtree_get_changed_ranges(
 
     // Compare the old and new subtrees.
     IteratorComparison comparison = iterator_compare(&old_iter, &new_iter);
+    if (realigned && comparison == IteratorMatches) comparison = IteratorMayDiffer;
+    realigned = false;
 
     // Even if the two subtrees appear to be identical, they could differ
     // internally if they contain a range of text that was previously
@@ -509,9 +512,11 @@ unsigned ts_subtree_get_changed_ranges(
     // Ensure that both iterators are at the same depth in the tree.
     while (old_iter.visible_depth > new_iter.visible_depth) {
       iterator_ascend(&old_iter);
+      realigned = true;
     }
     while (new_iter.visible_depth > old_iter.visible_depth) {
       iterator_ascend(&new_iter);
+      realigned = true;
     }
 
     if (is_changed) {
